<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Transportation – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Transportation</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Rules of the Road</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Talk about daily routines. Interactive: Participate in class discussions on everyday topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can talk about a daily routine involving transportation (e.g., "Every day, I take the bus to school at 5 a.m., and it's usually crowded." "I walk to school along the main road in my town every morning, and I cross the street at the crosswalk near the market." "I take a boat to school from my village in Portobelo, and the ride takes about 20 minutes.")
                Can participate in discussions about transportation preferences (e.g., "I take the bus because it's more affordable, but the metro is the easiest and fastest way to get around the city.")
                Can discuss road safety rules with specific examples (e.g., "We have to wear seat belts in the car to stay safe.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images showing various transportation types (bus, car, metro, boat, bicycle) and common traffic signs (stop sign, pedestrian crossing, speed limit). Students work in pairs to identify each image and verbally share one safety rule or characteristic associated with it. For example, "This is a bus, and passengers have to pay the fare." or "This is a stop sign; drivers have to stop here."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using target grammar and vocabulary, for instance, "We <b>should wear</b> a <b>seatbelt</b> in the <b>car</b> for <b>safety</b>." or "The <b>bus</b> is often <b>crowded</b> during <b>rush hour</b>." The teacher also models pronunciation, focusing on stress in words like "transportation," "convenience," "passenger," and distinguishing between long and short vowels in minimal pairs, e.g., /æ/ in "passenger" vs. /ɑː/ in "port." The teacher uses sentence examples like "The <b>passenger</b> <b>taps in</b> when <b>boarding</b> the bus."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a driver sees a red traffic light, what <b>do they have to do</b>?" (Expected: "They have to stop.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why <b>should</b> people use the <b>sidewalk</b> instead of the road?" (Expected: "For safety, to avoid cars.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Transportation."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording or written dialogue (presented on a projector) of two Panamanian teenagers, Mariana and Carlos, discussing their daily commute to school and emphasizing road safety. The dialogue includes comparisons of transportation options and mentions specific rules and protocols.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Participate in class discussions on everyday topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue Snippet:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mariana:</b> "Hey Carlos, how do you usually <b>get to</b> school?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Carlos:</b> "I usually <b>take the bus</b>. It's <b>more affordable</b> than a taxi, but sometimes it's really <b>crowded</b>. What about you?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mariana:</b> "I walk most days, but I always use the <b>sidewalk</b> and I <b>have to cross</b> at the <b>crosswalk</b>. My mom says <b>safety</b> is the <b>most important</b> thing. When I <b>get on the bus</b>, I always <b>tap in</b> with my metro card."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Carlos:</b> "That's smart. My dad always reminds me, 'You <b>should always wear</b> your <b>seatbelt</b> if you're in a car.' It's a key <b>rule of the road</b>."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to/read the dialogue once for gist, then a second time to answer specific questions orally in pairs:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What types of transportation do Mariana and Carlos use?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which transportation is more affordable according to Carlos?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What safety rules do they mention for walking and riding in a car?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Modal verbs for advice and obligation ("should," "have to"), Comparatives and Superlatives (e.g., "faster," "safest," "more convenient"), Phrasal verbs related to transportation ("get on," "get off," "tap in," "hop on"). Vocabulary: "road," "safety," "convenience," "crowded," "efficient," "fare," "schedule," "seatbelt," "traffic light," "sidewalk."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Modal Verbs & Rules):</b> The teacher provides sentence stems with blanks related to "Rules of the Road." Students complete them orally in pairs using "should" or "have to." For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You ___ stop at a red <b>traffic light</b>." (have to)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Pedestrians ___ use the <b>sidewalk</b> when available." (should)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Drivers ___ slow down near a school." (should)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Everyone ___ wear a <b>seatbelt</b> in a moving <b>vehicle</b>." (has to)</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher listens and corrects pronunciation and grammar.
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Practice (Comparatives/Superlatives):</b> Students receive cards with two or three transportation options (e.g., "bus," "taxi," "metro") and criteria (e.g., "cost," "speed," "convenience," "crowded"). In pairs, they orally compare and contrast these options using comparatives and superlatives. For example: "The metro is <b>faster than</b> the bus, but the taxi is the <b>most expensive</b>." "Taking the bus is <b>more affordable</b>, but it can be <b>more crowded</b>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction (Phrasal Verbs):</b> Students interview each other about their daily commute or typical journeys using the target phrasal verbs.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What time do you usually <b>get on</b> the bus/metro?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you <b>tap in</b> with a card or pay with cash?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where do you usually <b>get off</b>?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you ever <b>hop on</b> a taxi when you're late?"</li>
                        </ul>
                        Students provide feedback to their partners on clarity and usage. The teacher circulates, noting common errors and providing immediate oral feedback.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Transportation."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in pairs: "Advising a Tourist on Transportation and Safety in Panama."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. One student plays a tourist unfamiliar with local transportation and "Rules of the Road" in Panama. The other student plays a local guide.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The tourist asks questions about the best/most efficient/safest ways to travel to different places (e.g., "How <b>should I get</b> to the Panama Canal? Is the metro <b>faster than</b> the bus?").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The local guide provides advice, compares different <b>transportation</b> options (bus, metro, taxi, walking), and explains essential <b>safety rules</b> and local <b>protocols</b> (e.g., "You <b>have to wear</b> a <b>seatbelt</b> in taxis," "You <b>should avoid</b> walking alone at night," "Always use the <b>crosswalk</b>," "Remember to <b>tap in</b> when you <b>board</b> the metro.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to use modal verbs for advice and obligation, comparatives/superlatives, and the phrasal verbs introduced. They should also emphasize safety and make suggestions for convenience.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on oral production, active participation, and use of target language. Peer assessment is also integrated.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Advising a Tourist" role-plays in front of another pair or the class. The teacher uses an observation checklist to note:
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of modal verbs for advice/obligation (should, have to).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of comparatives and superlatives.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of transportation-related vocabulary and phrasal verbs.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of suggestions for convenience and emphasis on safety.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, including word stress and vowel contrasts.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active participation and collaborative interaction.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences or answer two questions orally/in writing before leaving:
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new safety rule I learned or discussed today is..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I think the __________ is the most __________ way to travel in Panama because __________."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel talking about different transportation options and rules of the road in English now?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of today's speaking activities, and how can you improve?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The speaking activities provided ample opportunity for students to practice modal verbs, comparatives, and transportation vocabulary in a communicative context. The role-play encouraged active interaction and critical thinking about safety and convenience. Some students still need more practice with precise pronunciation of multisyllabic words and consistently applying vowel contrasts. For next time, I need to provide more explicit feedback on specific pronunciation points. The pair work and group discussions worked well to increase speaking time.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4, students will use the vocabulary, grammar, and ideas discussed today to write descriptive paragraphs about their daily commute, transportation preferences, and important road safety rules, further solidifying their understanding and productive skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>